dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.


wm development configuration
----------------------------
This checkout is an unactivated customization. Use make test from the parent
wm directory, not make install or startx. It uses one tag, floating windows,
click-only focus, per-window half/quarter placement, maximize/restore and ordinary
close requests without killing unsupported clients. The manual documents the
current bindings, including the Plasma-style Super+Home, Super+PageUp/PageDown,
Super+End and Super+Shift+PageUp/PageDown media shortcuts. Session controls need
playerctl, wpctl (WirePlumber), brightnessctl, Flameshot and wm-launcher. Commands
use direct argument vectors; no shell or separate hotkey daemon is involved.

The native bar stays visible at the bottom of every monitor, reserving usable
space; fullscreen windows may cover it. Inicio opens an extensible Rofi menu.
The remaining area shows ungrouped tasks for that monitor, including minimized
windows. Click to activate/restore; click the focused task to minimize. Tags and
layout indicators are hidden. Titlebars default to off; showtitlebar in config.h
(or the config.def.h default) enables them after rebuilding.
Applications can move their windows by dragging their own header or free tab-strip
area when they send the standard _NET_WM_MOVERESIZE pointer-move request. No extra
titlebar or Super key is needed. Maximized/snapped windows restore floating size
under the pointer on actual motion; clicking alone does not restore them.
Super+left-drag uses the same movement path and remains the fallback for clients
without draggable headers. Fullscreen/docks/hidden windows are not moved.
The same protocol supports all eight pointer resize edges/corners, including
client size hints and fixed-size protection. The edge opposite a top/left grip
stays anchored after applying size hints. Resize from the current rectangle,
including maximized/snapped placement; a real size change clears that placement.
Application-requested drags never warp the pointer. Keyboard move/resize directions
are not implemented; the existing keyboard placement shortcuts remain available.
Status and the built-in XEmbed tray stay
on the primary RandR monitor (first monitor fallback), not the focused monitor.
The coordinator's docs/session.md describes the X11 session, snixembed SNI bridge,
clock, launcher, dependencies and coexistence with Plasma. dwm does not start the
compositor, bridge, network applet or screen locker itself. Synthetic tests do
not establish hardware, visual or authentication correctness.

The tray adapts the community suckless systray patch, including work by Jan
Christoph Ebersbach, Eon S. Jeon and subsequent contributors; the inspected
20260516 revision is by Bakkeby. The original diff, checksum and full provenance
are retained in the coordinator's patches/upstream/dwm/ and patches/README.md.
This version embeds the tray as a bar child, keeps icons separate from normal
clients, bounds icon sizes, honors hidden icons and does not steal another tray.
Native RandR events replace external panel polling/restarts. The private
_WM_SYSTRAY root CARDINAL pair (dwm PID, tray window) signals successful ownership
to the supervisor and is removed on cleanup or selection loss.

wm-displays also coordinates per-window geometry history with dwm through the
private _WM_DISPLAY_SETUP root property. Up to 16 arrangements are remembered
per open floating window, only in memory; no title-based matching or reopening.
Current fullscreen/snap states are kept, and docks/tiled clients are excluded.
Geometry is captured before monitor fallback, then restored after a stable setup.
No window history survives closing the window or restarting dwm.

The upstream installation instructions below are retained for reference only;
they are not the activation procedure for this environment.

Requirements
------------
This checkout needs Xlib, Xft, Fontconfig, Xinerama and Xrandr development files.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

In order to display status info in the bar, you can do something
like this in your .xinitrc:

    while xsetroot -name "`date` `uptime | sed 's/.*,//'`"
    do
    	sleep 1
    done &
    exec dwm


Configuration
-------------
The configuration of dwm is done by creating a custom config.h
and (re)compiling the source code.
